Efficient Selective-ID Secure Identity Based Encryption

Without Random Oracles

\begin{quote}
Dan Boneh\emph{∗}Xavier Boyen

Stanford University Voltage Security

\emph{dabo@cs.stanford.edu} \emph{xb@boyen.org}
\end{quote}

December 2004

\textbf{Abstract}

\begin{quote}
We construct two efficient Identity Based Encryption (IBE) systems that
are selective iden-tity secure \emph{without the random oracle model} in
groups equipped with a bilinear map. Selective identity secure IBE is a
slightly weaker security model than the standard security model for IBE.
In this model the adversary must commit ahead of time to the identity
that it intends to attack, whereas in the standard model the adversary
is allowed to choose this identity adaptively. The first system is based
on the decisional bilinear Diffie-Hellman assumption, and extends to
give a selective identity Hierarchical IBE secure without random
oracles. The second system is based on a related assumption called the
bilinear Diffie-Hellman inversion assumption. Applications of either
system include an efficient CCA2 public key cryptosystem that supports
non-interactive threshold decryption in the standard model, and a simple
and practical IBE system that remains secure against full adaptive-ID
attacks, under some security penalty, without random oracles.

\textbf{Keywords:} identity-based encryption, bilinear groups,
selective-ID security, full IBE security, CCA2 security, bilinear
Diffie-Hellman inversion, public-key encryption, probable security.

\textbf{1} \textbf{Introduction}

Identity Based Encryption (IBE) provides a public key encryption
mechanism where a public key is an arbitrary string such as an email
address or a telephone number. The corresponding private key can only be
generated by a Private Key Generator (PKG) who has knowledge of a master
secret. In an IBE system, users authenticate themselves to the PKG and
obtain private keys corresponding to their identities. The identity
based encryption model was first proposed without solution two decades
ago {[}Sha84{]}, and several different approaches were subsequently
suggested in a few precursor papers {[}Tan87, TI89, MY96{]}. It is only
a few years ago, however, that a formal security model and a practical
implementation were proposed. Boneh and Franklin {[}BF01, BF03{]} define
a security model for identity based encryption and gave a construction
based on the Bilinear Diffie-Hellman (BDH) problem. Cocks {[}Coc01{]}
describes another construction using quadratic residues modulo a
composite, which however is less practical than the Boneh-Franklin
system. The security of these systems requires cryptographic hash
functions that are modeled as random oracles, i.e., these systems are
only proven secure in the random oracle model {[}BR93{]}. A natural
question is to devise a secure IBE system without random oracles.

\emph{∗}Supported by NSF and the Packard Foundation.
\end{quote}

1

In the Boneh-Franklin security model the adversary can issue both
adaptive chosen ciphertext queries and adaptive chosen identity queries
(i.e., the adversary can request the private key for identities of its
choice). Eventually, the adversary adaptively chooses the identity it
wishes to attack and asks for a semantic security challenge for this
identity. Canetti et al. {[}CHK03, CHK04{]} recently proposed a slightly
weaker security model, called selective identity IBE. In this model the
adversary must commit ahead of time (non-adaptively) to the identity it
intends to attack. The adversary can still issue adaptive chosen
ciphertext and adaptive chosen identity queries. Canetti et al. are able
to construct a provably secure IBE in this weaker model without the
random oracle model. However, their construction views identities as bit
strings, causing their system to require a bilinear map computation for
every bit in the identity.

We construct two efficient IBE systems that are provably selective
identity secure without the random oracle model. In both systems,
encryption requires no bilinear map computation and decryption requires
at most two. Our first construction is based on the Decision Bilinear
Diffie-Hellman (Decision BDH) assumption. This construction extends to
give an efficient selective identity secure Hierarchical IBE (HIBE)
without random oracles. Hierarchical IBE was defined in {[}HL02{]} and
the first construction in the random oracle model was given by Gentry
and Silver-berg {[}GS02{]}. Our efficient HIBE construction is similar
to the Gentry-Silverberg system, but we are able to prove security
without using random oracles. Our second IBE construction is even more
efficient, but is based on a non-standard assumption we call Decision
Bilinear Diffie-Hellman Inversion (Decision BDHI). Roughly speaking, the
assumption says that no efficient algorithm can distinguish
\emph{e}(\emph{g, g})1\emph{/x}from random, given \emph{g, gx,
g}(\emph{x}2)\emph{, . . . , g}(\emph{xq})for some \emph{q}.

Canetti et al. {[}CHK04{]} recently showed that any selective identity,
chosen plaintext IBE gives a chosen ciphertext secure (CCA2) public key
system. Consequently, both our IBE systems give efficient CCA2-secure
public key systems without random oracles. Performance of both these
CCA2-secure systems is comparable to the performance of the Cramer-Shoup
system {[}CS98{]} which is based on Decision Diffie-Hellman.

Towards our goal of providing a practical secure IBE system without
random oracle, we show that any selective-ID secure IBE can be turned
into a fully secure IBE in the standard model, albeit at the cost of an
inefficient security reduction. We note that Boneh and Boyen {[}BB04{]}
recently gave the first fully secure IBE construction that is polynomial
in all parameters, a construction which was subsequently simplified and
made much more efficient by Waters {[}Wat04{]}. For practical purposes,
the transformation from efficient selective-ID IBE systems presented
here results in even simpler and more efficient systems: indeed, the
large security penalty incurred by the transformation is easily
compensated for by increasing the size of the groups used by the system.
The result are two very simple secure IBE systems without random
oracles.

\textbf{2} \textbf{Preliminaries}

Before presenting our results we briefly review the definition of
security for an IBE system. We also review the definition of groups
equipped with a bilinear map.

\textbf{2.1 Selective Identity Secure IBE and HIBE Systems}

Recall that an Identity Based Encryption system (IBE) consists of four
algorithms {[}Sha84, BF01{]}: \emph{Setup}, \emph{KeyGen},
\emph{Encrypt}, \emph{Decrypt}. The \emph{Setup} algorithm generates
system parameters, denoted by \emph{params}, and a master key
\emph{master-key}. The \emph{KeyGen} algorithm uses the master key to
generate the private key corresponding to a given identity. The
encryption algorithm encrypts messages for a

2

given identity (using the system parameters) and the decryption
algorithm decrypts ciphertexts using the private key. In a Hierarchical
IBE {[}HL02, GS02{]} identities are vectors. A vector of dimension
\emph{ℓ}represents an identity at depth \emph{ℓ}. Algorithm
\emph{KeyGen} takes as input an identity ID = (I1\emph{, . . . ,}
I\emph{ℓ}) at depth \emph{ℓ}and the private key
\emph{d}ID\emph{\textbar ℓ−}1 of the parent identity
ID\emph{\textbar ℓ−}1 = (I1\emph{, . . . ,} I\emph{ℓ−}1) at depth
\emph{ℓ−}1. It outputs the private key \emph{d}ID for identity ID. We
refer to the \emph{master-key} as the private key at depth 0 and note
that an IBE system is an HIBE where all identities are at depth 1. Boneh
and Franklin {[}BF01, BF03{]} define chosen ciphertext security for IBE
systems under a chosen identity attack. In their model the adversary is
allowed to adaptively chose the public key it wishes to attack (the
public key on which it will be challenged). Canetti, Halevi, and Katz
{[}CHK03, CHK04{]} define a weaker notion of security in which the
adversary commits ahead of time to the public key it will attack. We
refer to this notion as selective identity, chosen ciphertext secure IBE
(IND-sID-CCA). More precisely, selective identity IBE and HIBE security
is defined using the following game:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Init:} The adversary outputs an identity ID\emph{∗}where it
wishes to be challenged.

\textbf{Setup:} The challenger runs the \emph{Setup} algorithm. It gives
the adversary the resulting system parameters \emph{params}. It keeps
the \emph{master-key} to itself.

\textbf{Phase 1:} The adversary issues queries \emph{q}1\emph{, . . . ,
qm} where query \emph{qi} is one of:

-- Private key query \emph{⟨}ID\emph{i⟩}where ID\emph{i ̸}=
ID\emph{∗}and ID\emph{i} is not a prefix of ID\emph{∗}. The challenger
responds by running algorithm \emph{KeyGen} to generate the private key
\emph{di} corresponding to the public key \emph{⟨}ID\emph{i⟩}. It sends
\emph{di} to the adversary.

-- Decryption query \emph{⟨Ci⟩}for identity ID\emph{∗}or any prefix of
ID\emph{∗}. The challenger responds by running algorithm \emph{KeyGen}
to generate the private key \emph{d} corresponding to ID\emph{∗}(or the
relevant prefix thereof as requested). It then runs algorithm
\emph{Decrypt} to decrypt the ciphertext \emph{Ci} using the private key
\emph{d}. It sends the resulting plaintext to the adversary.

These queries may be asked adaptively, that is, each query \emph{qi} may
depend on the replies to \emph{q}1\emph{, . . . , qi−}1.

\textbf{Challenge:} Once the adversary decides that Phase 1 is over it
outputs two equal length plaintexts \emph{M}0\emph{, M}1 \emph{∈M} on
which it wishes to be challenged. The challenger picks a random bit
\emph{b ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}} and sets the challenge ciphertext to
\emph{C} = \emph{Encrypt}(\emph{params,} ID\emph{∗, Mb}). It sends
\emph{C} as the challenge to the adversary.

\textbf{Phase 2:} The adversary issues additional queries
\emph{qm}+1\emph{, . . . , qn} where \emph{qi} is one of:

-- Private key query \emph{⟨}ID\emph{i⟩}where ID\emph{i ̸}=
ID\emph{∗}and ID\emph{i} is not a prefix of ID\emph{∗}. The challenger
responds as in Phase 1.

-- Decryption query \emph{⟨Ci⟩̸}= \emph{⟨C⟩}for ID\emph{∗}or any prefix
of ID\emph{∗}. The challenger responds as in Phase 1.

These queries may be asked adaptively as in Phase 1.

\textbf{Guess:} Finally, the adversary outputs a guess
\emph{b′∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}}. The adversary wins if \emph{b} =
\emph{b′}.
\end{quote}

We refer to such an adversary \emph{A} as an IND-sID-CCA adversary. We
define the advantage of the adversary \emph{A} in attacking the scheme
\emph{E} as

\begin{quote}
Adv\emph{E,A} = Pr{[}\emph{b} = \emph{b′}{]} \emph{−}1
\end{quote}

The probability is over the random bits used by the challenger and the
adversary.

3

\textbf{Definition 2.1.} We say that an IBE or HIBE system \emph{E} is
(\emph{t, q}ID\emph{, qC, ϵ})-selective identity, adaptive chosen
ciphertext secure if for any \emph{t}-time IND-sID-CCA adversary
\emph{A} that makes at most \emph{q}ID chosen private key queries and at
most \emph{qC} chosen decryption queries we have that Adv\emph{E,A
\textless{} ϵ}. As

shorthand, we say that \emph{E} is (\emph{t, q}ID\emph{, qC, ϵ})
IND-sID-CCA secure.

\textbf{Semantic Security.} As usual, we define selective identity,
chosen plaintext security for an IBE

system as in the preceding game, except that the adversary is not
allowed to issue any decryption

queries. The adversary may still issue adaptive private key queries.

\textbf{Definition 2.2.} We say that an IBE or HIBE system \emph{E} is
(\emph{t, q}ID\emph{, ϵ})-selective identity, chosen plaintext secure if
\emph{E} is (\emph{t, q}ID\emph{,} 0\emph{, ϵ})-selective identity,
chosen ciphertext secure. As shorthand, we say that \emph{E} is
(\emph{t, q}ID\emph{, ϵ}) IND-sID-CPA secure.

\textbf{2.2 Bilinear Groups}

We briefly review the necessary facts about bilinear maps and bilinear
map groups {[}Jou00, Mil04{]}.

We use the following notation:

\begin{quote}
1. G and G1 are two (multiplicative) cyclic groups of prime order
\emph{p};

2. \emph{g} is a generator of G.

3. \emph{e} is a bilinear map \emph{e} : G \emph{×} G \emph{→}G1.
\end{quote}

following properties: Let G and G1 be two groups as above. A bilinear
map is a map \emph{e} : G \emph{×} G \emph{→}G1 with the

\begin{quote}
1. Bilinearity: for all \emph{u, v ∈}G and \emph{a, b ∈}Z, we have
\emph{e}(\emph{ua, vb}) = \emph{e}(\emph{u, v})\emph{ab}. 2.
Non-degeneracy: \emph{e}(\emph{g, g}) \emph{̸}= 1.

We say that G is a bilinear group if the group action in G can be
computed efficiently and there
\end{quote}

exists a group G1 and an efficiently computable bilinear map \emph{e} :
G \emph{×} G \emph{→}G1 as above. Note that \emph{e}(\emph{,} ) is
symmetric since \emph{e}(\emph{ga, gb}) = \emph{e}(\emph{g,
g})\emph{ab}= \emph{e}(\emph{gb, ga}).

Throughout the paper, for a prime order group G we use G\emph{∗}to
denote the set G \emph{\textbackslash{} \{}1G\emph{\}} where 1G is the
identity of G.

\textbf{3} \textbf{Complexity Assumptions}

Let G be a bilinear group of prime order \emph{p}. We review the
standard Bilinear Diffie-Hellman (BDH)

assumption and define the Bilinear Diffie-Hellman Inversion (BDHI)
assumption.

\textbf{3.1 Bilinear Diffie-Hellman Assumption}

