\begin{quote}
Occasionally we drop the \emph{t} and \emph{ϵ} and refer to the
\emph{q}-BDHI and Decision \emph{q}-BDHI assumptions.
\end{quote}

It is not known if the \emph{q}-BDHI assumption, for \emph{q
\textgreater{}} 1, is equivalent to BDH. A closely related

assumption was previously used in {[}MSK02{]} where it was called weak
Diffie-Hellman.

\textbf{4} \textbf{Efficient Selective Identity IBE and HIBE Based on
BDH}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Without Random Oracles}
\end{quote}

We construct an efficient HIBE system that is selective identity secure
without random oracles

based on the Decision BDH assumption. In particular, this implies an
efficient selective identity,

chosen ciphertext secure IBE based on Decision BDH without random
oracles.

\textbf{4.1 Construction}

Let G be a bilinear group of prime order \emph{p} (the security
parameter determines the size of G). Let

\emph{e} : G \emph{×} G \emph{→}G1 be the bilinear map. For now, we
assume public keys (ID) of depth \emph{ℓ}are vectors of elements in
Z\emph{pℓ}. We write ID = (I1\emph{, . . . ,} I\emph{ℓ})
\emph{∈}Z\emph{pℓ}. The \emph{j}-th component corresponds to the
identity at level \emph{j}. We later extend the construction to public
keys over \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}by first hashing each component
I\emph{j} using a collision resistant hash \emph{H} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}∗→}Z\emph{p}. We also assume messages to be encrypted are
elements in G1. The HIBE system works as follows:

