The HIBE system above is reminiscent of the Gentry-Silverberg HIBE which
is only known to be

secure in the random oracle model. Surprisingly, our choice of functions
\emph{F}1\emph{, . . . , Fℓ}enables us to

prove security \emph{without random oracles}. We prove security of our
HIBE system under the standard

Decision BDH assumption in G.

\textbf{Theorem 4.1.} \emph{Suppose the} (\emph{t, ϵ})\emph{-Decision
BDH assumption holds in} G\emph{. Then the previously defined}

\emph{ℓ-HIBE system is} (\emph{t′, qS, ϵ})\emph{-selective identity,
chosen plaintext} (IND-sID-CPA) \emph{secure for arbitrary ℓ}

\emph{and qS, and any t′\textless{} t −}Θ(\emph{τ ℓqS}) \emph{where τ is
the maximum time for an exponentiation in} G\emph{.}

