A recent result of Canetti et al. {[}CHK04{]} gives an efficient way to
build a selective identity, chosen

ciphertext \emph{ℓ}-HIBE from a selective identity, chosen plaintext
(\emph{ℓ}+ 1)-HIBE. In combination with

the above construction, we obtain a selective identity, chosen
ciphertext \emph{ℓ}-HIBE for any \emph{ℓ}. In

particular, from our 2-HIBE we obtain an efficient selective identity,
chosen ciphertext secure IBE

without random oracles.

\textbf{4.4 Arbitrary Identities}

We can extend our HIBE above to handle identities ID = (I1\emph{, . . .
,} I\emph{ℓ}) with I\emph{j ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}(as opposed

to \emph{Ij ∈}Z\emph{p}) by first hashing each \emph{Ij} using a
collision resistant hash function \emph{H} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}∗→}Z\emph{p} prior to key generation and encryption. A standard
argument shows that if the scheme above is selective

identity, chosen ciphertext secure then so is the scheme with the
additional hash function. We note

that there is no need for a full domain hash into Z\emph{p}; for
example, a collision resistant hash function

\emph{H} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→\{}1\emph{, . . . ,}
2\emph{b\}} where 2\emph{b\textless{} p} is sufficient for the security
proof.

\textbf{4.5 Fast Key Generation}

It is worth noting that in the practical case of a non-hierarchical IBE
system without secret sharing,

the \emph{KeyGen} algorithm of Section 4.1 can be implemented very
efficiently. The implementation

makes use of the discrete logarithms of \emph{g}1 and \emph{h}1, which
is accommodated by slightly modifying

the \emph{Setup} algorithm to select \emph{h}1 \emph{←gη}where \emph{η
∈}Z\emph{p} is random, and to preserve the (secret) values of \emph{α}
and \emph{η} along with the master key \emph{gα}2for future use. Under
these assumptions, the expression

of the private key \emph{d}ID returned by \emph{KeyGen} for a given
identity ID can be calculated as follows:

\begin{quote}
\emph{d}ID = \emph{gα}2\emph{hr} 1\emph{gr} ID \emph{, gr} =
\emph{gα}2\emph{· g}(\emph{η}+\emph{α} ID) \emph{r, gr} =
\emph{master-key · gξ, gr}
\end{quote}

Since \emph{master-key} = \emph{gα}2is constant, the only variable
quantities in the last expression are the two

powers \emph{gξ}and \emph{gr}of the same fixed base \emph{g} to the
known exponents \emph{r} and \emph{ξ} = (\emph{η}+\emph{α}ID)\emph{r}
respectively.

Without precomputation, the cost of jointly computing both quantities
slightly exceeds that of a

single exponentiation in G, which for the sake of comparison is also the
cost of key generation in

8

the Boneh-Franklin IBE system. Furthermore, since here the
exponentiation base \emph{g} is fixed, pre-computation techniques can be
exploited to further reduce the total cost to a fraction of that level.
For example, using an exponentiation algorithm with \emph{W}-bit
windows, the asymptotic amortized cost of key extraction is found to be
about \emph{W}+1that of the Boneh-Franklin system, i.e., a threefold
speedup for the common choice of 5-bit windows.

\textbf{5} \textbf{More Efficient Selective Identity IBE Based on BDHI}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Without Random Oracles}
\end{quote}

We construct an efficient IBE system that is selective identity, chosen
plaintext secure without random oracles based on the Decision
\emph{q}-BDHI assumption (see Section 3.2). Decryption in the resulting
IBE system is more efficient that the IBE construction in the previous
section. Encryption efficiency and ciphertext size are the same.

\textbf{5.1 Basic Construction}

Let G be a bilinear group of prime order \emph{p}. For now, we assume
that the public keys (ID) are elements in Z\emph{∗p}. We show later that
arbitrary identities in \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}can be used by
first hashing ID using a collision resistant hash \emph{H} :
\emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→}Z\emph{∗p}. We also assume that the
messages to be encrypted are elements in G1. The IBE system works as
follows:

\textbf{\emph{Setup}:} To generate IBE parameters, select a random
generator \emph{g ∈}G\emph{∗}, select random elements \emph{x, y
∈}Z\emph{∗master-key} are given by \emph{p}, and define \emph{X} =
\emph{gx} and \emph{Y} = \emph{gy}. The public parameters \emph{params}
and the secret

\begin{quote}
\emph{params} = (\emph{g, X, Y} ) \emph{,} \emph{master-key} = (\emph{x,
y})
\end{quote}

\textbf{\emph{KeyGen}(\emph{master-key},ID):} To create a private key
for the public key ID \emph{∈}Z\emph{∗p}:

\begin{quote}
1. pick a random \emph{r ∈}Z\emph{p} and compute \emph{K} =
\emph{g}1\emph{/}(ID+\emph{x}+\emph{ry})\emph{∈}G, 2. output the private
key \emph{d}ID = (\emph{r, K}).

In the unlikely event that \emph{x} + \emph{ry} + ID = 0 (mod \emph{p}),
try again with a new random value for \emph{r}.
\end{quote}

\textbf{\emph{Encrypt}(\emph{params}, ID, M):} To encrypt a message
\emph{M ∈}G1 under public key ID \emph{∈}Z\emph{∗p}, pick a random
