\textbf{Fully Secure IBE Using Random Oracles.} It is also worth noting
that a random oracle \emph{H}

immediately converts a selective-ID IBE \emph{E} to a fully secure IBE
by the process of hashing the identity ID with \emph{H} before using ID.
We denote the resulting system by \emph{EH}. We state this in the
following theorem.

\textbf{Theorem 7.2.} \emph{Let E be a} (\emph{t, qS, ϵ})
\emph{selective-ID secure IBE. Suppose identities in E are n-bits long.
Let H be a hash function H} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}nmodeled as a random oracle. Then EH is a} (\emph{t, qS, ϵ′})
\emph{fully secure IBE (in the random oracle model) for ϵ′}= \emph{ϵ ·
qH/}(1 \emph{−qS/}2\emph{n}) \emph{≈qH · ϵ, where qH is the maximum
number of oracle calls to H that the adversary can make.}

\begin{quote}
The proof of the theorem is similar to the proof of Theorem 7.1 and is
omitted. We merely
\end{quote}

mention that the additional correction factor
(1\emph{−qS/}2\emph{n})\emph{−}1accounts for the possible random oracle
collisions. It is worth noting that in the proof, the random oracle is
``programmed'' at only one

point. Unlike the original Boneh-Franklin IBE scheme and its many
variants, where the random

oracle programmability is crucial to answer all private key queries,
here the random oracle is only

needed to ensure that the hash of the challenge identity provided by the
adversary (ID\emph{∗}0) is mapped

to the identity chosen by the simulator at the initialization step
(ID\emph{∗}).

A consequence of Theorems 7.1 and 7.2 is that, using a collision
resistant function \emph{H} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}n}for a properly chosen \emph{n} satisfying
2160\emph{≤}2\emph{n≪p}, the same hash IBE scheme \emph{EH} can be
proven secure in both the standard model and the random oracle model.
Specifically, the scheme

features (\emph{t, qS,} 2\emph{nϵ})-full IBE security in the standard
model, which is boosted to (\emph{t, qS, qHϵ}) when

\emph{H} is viewed as a random oracle.

