\textbf{8} \textbf{Arbitrary Length Identities Without Collision
Resistance}

We mentioned in Sections 4.4 and 5.4 that both schemes can easily be
extended to accept arbitrary

length strings as public keys, using collision resistant hashing. It is
well known that collision

resistant hash functions can be constructed in groups where the discrete
logarithm problem is hard;

which is clearly the case under the present assumptions. However, in
practice, it is advantageous

to use faster hash constructions such as SHA-1, whose collision
resistance rests mainly on faith. In

this section we ask whether our IBE systems can be extended to accept
arbitrary length identities

using a weaker assumption than collision resistance. We observe that a
universal one-way hash

family (UOWHF) of functions \emph{Hk} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→I}
is sufficient to transform any selective-ID secure IBE with a finite
identity set \emph{I} into a selective-ID secure IBE that takes
arbitrary length identities in \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}.

Recall that a (\emph{t′, ϵ′})-UOWHF {[}NY89{]} is a family of functions
\emph{Hk} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→I} with indices in \emph{K},
such that, for any randomized algorithm (\emph{A}1\emph{, A}2) running
in time \emph{t′}, it holds that:

\begin{quote}
Pr \emph{Hk}(\emph{x}) = \emph{Hk}(\emph{x′}) : (\emph{x, σ})
\emph{←A}1\emph{, k}R\emph{←K, x′←A}2(\emph{x, k, σ}) \emph{\textless{}
ϵ′}
\end{quote}

The probability is taken over the random choice of \emph{k} and the
random coins used by (\emph{A}1\emph{, A}2). For practical purposes, it
is assumed that the index \emph{k} ranges in a finite set \emph{K} and
has a compact representation.

Let \emph{E} be an IBE system that admits identities ID \emph{∈I}.
Suppose that \emph{H} = \emph{\{Hk\}} is a (\emph{t, ϵ′})-UOWHF of
functions \emph{Hk} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→I} indexed by
\emph{k ∈K}. We construct a new IBE system \emph{E′}that takes arbitrary
length identities ID \emph{∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}as follows:

\emph{\textbf{Setup}E′} runs \emph{SetupE} to obtain \emph{params} and
\emph{master-key}, and appends a random \emph{k ∈K} to give
\emph{params′}= (\emph{params, k}). The new public \emph{params′}and the
original \emph{master-key} are returned.

