\textbf{9} \textbf{DHI and Generalized Diffie-Hellman}

In Section 3.2 we defined the \emph{q}-BDHI problem in a bilinear group.
A closely related problem is the

\emph{q}-Diffie-Hellman Inversion (\emph{q}-DHI) problem: given a tuple
(\emph{g, gx, g}(\emph{x}2)\emph{, . . . , g}(\emph{xq}))
\emph{∈}G\emph{q}+1as input, output \emph{g}1\emph{/x∈}G. Here, G need
not be a bilinear group. Loosely speaking, the \emph{q}-DHI assumption
states that the \emph{q}-DHI problem is intractable in G. This
assumption was previously used in {[}MSK02{]}

where it was called weak Diffie-Hellman.

16

\begin{quote}
Many cryptographic constructions rely on the Generalized Diffie-Hellman
assumption (GenDH) for security {[}STW96, NR97, BBR99, Lys02, BS03{]}.
In this section we show that the \emph{q}-DHI as-sumption implies the
(\emph{q} + 1)-Generalized Diffie-Hellman assumption. Thus,
constructions that rely on Generalized Diffie-Hellman could instead rely
on \emph{q}-DHI which appears to be a more natural complexity
assumption, and is easier to state since the problem description does
not require an oracle.

We first review the GenDH assumption. The assumption says that, for a
random generator Q \emph{i∈Sai∈}G for any strict \emph{g} of G, given
\emph{ga}1\emph{, . . . , gaq}in G and given all the subset products
\emph{g}\\
