\emph{Og,⃗}with \emph{a}to be an oracle that for any strict subset
\emph{S ⊂\{}1\emph{, . . . , q\}} responds

\emph{Og,⃗a}(\emph{S}) = \emph{g} Q \emph{i∈Sai∈}G\emph{.}

Define the advantage of algorithm \emph{A} in solving the generalized
Diffie-Hellman problem to be the probability that \emph{A} is able to
compute \emph{ga}1\emph{···aq}given access to the oracle
\emph{Og,⃗a}(\emph{S}). In other words,

\begin{quote}
Adv\emph{A,q} = Pr{[}\emph{AOg,⃗a} = \emph{ga}1\emph{···aq} : \emph{g
←}G\emph{∗, ⃗a} = (\emph{a}1\emph{, . . . , aq}) \emph{←}Z\emph{pq}{]}

Note that the oracle only answers queries for strict subsets of
\emph{\{}1\emph{, . . . , q\}}.

\textbf{Definition 9.1.} We say that G satisfies the (\emph{t, q,
ϵ})-Generalized Diffie-Hellman assumption if for all \emph{t}-time
algorithms \emph{A} we have Adv\emph{A,q \textless{} ϵ}.

