The same property as in Theorem 9.2 also holds for the decision versions
of the DHI and GenDH problems. The \emph{q}-DHI assumption is easier to
state than the \emph{q}-GenDH assumption since there is no need for an
oracle. When appropriate, constructions that depend on GenDH for
security could instead use the DHI assumption.

\textbf{10} \textbf{Conclusions}

We constructed two IBE systems that are secure against selective
identity attacks in the standard model, i.e., without using random
oracles. The first construction is based on the now classic BDH
assumption. It extends readily to give a selective identity HIBE without
random oracles, that can efficiently be made chosen ciphertext secure
using a technique of {[}CHK04{]}. The second construction is based on
the Bilinear Diffie-Hellman Inversion assumption. The same technique of
{[}CHK04, BK04{]} converts both our constructions into efficient
CCA2-secure public key systems without random oracles that are almost as
efficient as the Cramer-Shoup public key system.

We observed that a selective-ID secure IBE system implies a fully secure
IBE system. Although the resulting security reduction is not polynomial,
the parameters can easily be dimensioned in such a way as to result in a
fully secure practical IBE system in the standard model, that is
computationally as efficient, within a constant factor, as its random
oracle counterpart of equivalent security.

Using the first construction in this paper as a building block, we were
recently able to construct the first fully secure IBE system without the
random oracle model with an efficient security reduc-tion {[}BB04{]},
thereby resolving an open problem posed in {[}BF01{]}. However, that
system is quite impractical and should only be viewed as a constructive
proof that such constructions are indeed possible. Waters {[}Wat04{]}
recently described an elegant generalization of this building block that
directly gives a more efficient fully secure IBE without the random
oracle model. The question of constructing a fully secure IBE system
with a tight reduction in the standard model remains open.

\textbf{Acknowledgments}

The comment on threshold systems in Section 6 came out of a discussion
with Shai Halevi. We also thank Shai Halevi and Jonathan Katz for
helpful comments on this work.

